{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_cklpigrxadqfthdoscu4komyuvkjcrs2vmz4tdki5ob36426s3xq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four phases are complete and the gate passes.\n\n## Summary\n\n**Phase A — builds.** Both programs build clean:\n- C: `cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .` → `c_src/build/driver`\n- Rust: `cd translation && cargo build --release` → `translation/target/release/driver` (compiled with no errors; `cargo clippy` reports 0 warnings)\n\n**Phase B/C — what the C actually does.** `main` does `int x = 0; scanf(\"%d\", &x); if (x) good(); else bad();`. Both `good()` and `bad()` copy a zero-filled `source[10]` into `data` and print `data[0]` — so **every** run prints exactly `0\\n` and exits 0. The `if (x)` branch is not observable in output; only formatting, exit status, and signal behavior can differ. Notably `bad()` does `alloca(10)` then writes 40 bytes (UB); I confirmed with gcc 11.5 both plain and with `-O2 -fstack-protector-strong -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=2` that it neither crashes nor changes output, and reproduced only the observable behavior rather than the UB.\n\nI wrote `translation/tests/differential.rs` — 17 tests that spawn **both binaries** as subprocesses and compare stdout, stderr, and exit status (including termination signal). Coverage: empty/closed stdin, `0` variants, non-zero variants, scanf matching failures (`abc`, `.5`, bare `-`/`+`, `0x10`), whitespace skipping across newlines (`scanf` vs `fgets` semantics), int boundaries, values that truncate to zero in an `int` (2³², 2³²+1), 400-digit saturation, conversion stopping at the first non-digit, ignored trailing input, NUL/high/all-256 bytes, and 25× determinism runs.\n\n**One real mismatch found and fixed** (recorded in `translation/ERRORS.md`):\n\n> **Broken stdout pipe.** With a closed reader, C died silently from `SIGPIPE` (signal 13, empty stderr) while Rust printed a panic message to stderr and aborted (`SIGABRT`) — a mismatch on both stderr *and* exit status. Cause: the Rust runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`, so the failing write returned `EPIPE` and `println!` panicked (turned into `SIGABRT` by `panic = \"abort\"`). Fix: `main` now calls `restore_default_sigpipe()` to reinstate `SIG_DFL`. Both now die with signal 13 and empty stderr.\n\n**Non-vacuity of the suite** — I verified the tests can actually fail, by mutating scratch copies of the crate: changing the exit code, dropping the trailing newline, and printing `1` instead of `data[0]` each fail 16/16 tests; removing the SIGPIPE fix fails exactly the broken-pipe test.\n\n**Extra assurance:** a randomized sweep of 795 inputs (random bytes, numeric-ish tokens, boundary values with assorted whitespace) produced 0 mismatches.\n\n**Gate:** both build ✅ · all enumerated inputs identical on stdout/stderr/status ✅ · `cargo test` passes in both debug and release (17 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored) ✅ · nothing disabled or `#[ignore]`d ✅ · `c_src/` sources unmodified (only the generated `build/` dir added) ✅"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":810,"cache_read_input_tokens":43488,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":810,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6aab9c7e-8f84-4469-b45d-52fe0c19db00","uuid":"2eff969e-d274-4775-b3e7-2cb2d086c616","timestamp":"2026-08-28T13:10:30.365Z"}
